{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md | sed -n '9,236p'\"","aggregated_output":"     9\t## 1. Public API\n    10\t\n    11\tThe declarations are in [packages/axylo/src/public.ts](../packages/axylo/src/public.ts), and every example below is in [docs/api/examples.ts](api/examples.ts), type-checked against the package by `npm run typecheck`: the examples compile, and the deliberate mistakes fail to compile. (The first revision of this plan carried a separate declaration sketch, `docs/api/axylo.d.ts`; the package replaced it.)\n    12\t\n    13\t### 1.1 Shape\n    14\t\n    15\tOne function per chart type, each taking a target element and a plain options object, each returning the same small handle.\n    16\t\n    17\t```ts\n    18\timport { line } from 'axylo';\n    19\t\n    20\tconst chart = line('#sessions', {\n    21\t  title: 'Daily sessions by channel',\n    22\t  data: rows,              // [{ day: '2026-01-01', channel: 'Direct', sessions: 31713 }, …]\n    23\t  x: 'day',\n    24\t  y: 'sessions',\n    25\t  series: 'channel',\n    26\t});\n    27\t```\n    28\t\n    29\tThat is the whole first chart. Axes, legend, tooltip, keyboard access, description, data table, light and dark colors and resizing are on without further options.\n    30\t\n    31\t| Decision | Reason |\n    32\t|---|---|\n    33\t| Field names over a table | Finding D2: the ten libraries take five incompatible shapes, and the harness had to reshape its fixture a different way for each. Application data arrives as rows. |\n    34\t| One function per chart type (`line`, `area`, `bar`, `donut`, `scatter`) | Each has its own options type, so options that do not apply are type errors (DX3), and bundlers can drop unused chart types. Whether shared code actually tree-shakes is measured on real consumer builds in M7. |\n    35\t| Plain options object, not a builder or a JSX tree | The same object works in the React adapter. Recharts needed eight components for the trend task; the information content was four fields. |\n    36\t| No required configuration beyond data and fields | Findings R11, R15, D1: tooltips, legends, time adapters, margins, colors and accessibility were opt-in somewhere in every library. |\n    37\t\n    38\t### 1.2 Data: one logical table, three forms\n    39\t\n    40\t```ts\n    41\t// long rows: one row per (x, series)\n    42\tline(el, { data: rows, x: 'day', y: 'sessions', series: 'channel' });\n    43\t\n    44\t// wide rows: one column per series\n    45\tline(el, { data: wide, x: 'day', y: ['direct', 'organic', { field: 'referral', label: 'Referral' }] });\n    46\t\n    47\t// typed columns for large data; the row type is inferred from the columns\n    48\tline(el, { data: { columns: { t: new Float64Array(n), v: new Float32Array(n) } }, x: { field: 't', type: 'time' }, y: 'v' });\n    49\t```\n    50\t\n    51\tLong and wide are mutually exclusive in the types: `series` cannot be combined with an array of `y`.\n    52\t\n    53\tRules the three forms share:\n    54\t\n    55\t| Rule | Detail |\n    56\t|---|---|\n    57\t| Ownership | Row arrays are read once and never mutated. Typed columns are borrowed, not copied; the caller must not change them in place, and replaces them through `update`. |\n    58\t| Column lengths | All columns must have the same length; otherwise AX100. |\n    59\t| Missing values | Rows: `null`, or an absent key. Typed columns: `NaN` means missing and does not raise AX103. |\n    60\t| Order | Line, area and scatter are sorted by x internally (F11). `append` expects rows at or after the last x. |\n    61\t| Provenance | Every normalized sample keeps the index of the row it came from, so diagnostics, the table and events point at the caller's own data. |\n    62\t\n    63\t### 1.3 Fields\n    64\t\n    65\tA field is a key of the row, or an object when it needs more. The key is checked against the row type by role: a value axis accepts number fields; text fields are accepted there only with `parse: 'number'`.\n    66\t\n    67\t```ts\n    68\tline(el, {\n    69\t  data: samples,\n    70\t  x: { field: 'ts', type: 'time', timeZone: 'UTC' },\n    71\t  y: { field: 'sessions', label: 'Sessions', unit: 'sessions', tickFormat: { notation: 'compact' } },\n    72\t});\n    73\t\n    74\t// counts that a database driver returns as strings\n    75\tbar(el, { data: pgRows, x: 'day', y: { field: 'count', parse: 'number', missing: 'zero' } });\n    76\t```\n    77\t\n    78\t| Option | Meaning |\n    79\t|---|---|\n    80\t| `field` | Key of the row. |\n    81\t| `label`, `unit` | Axis title; name and unit in tooltip, table and announcements. |\n    82\t| `type` (x) | `'date'`, `'time'`, `'number'`, `'category'`. Inferred when omitted, except that a number is never inferred to be an instant (F4). |\n    83\t| `timeZone` (x) | For instants: `'local'` (default), `'UTC'`, or an IANA zone. |\n    84\t| `format`, `tickFormat` | Readouts and ticks are formatted separately (F6). |\n    85\t| `scale` (value) | `'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1). Declared, never inferred (F7). |\n    86\t| `missing` (value) | `'gap'` (default) or `'zero'` (F2). |\n    87\t| `parse` (value) | `'number'`, for a text field holding numbers (AX104, AX106). |\n    88\t| `domain` | Author bounds. Widened to zero on bar and area (F1). |\n    89\t\n    90\t### 1.4 Common options\n    91\t\n    92\tSummarized; the declarations are the reference.\n    93\t\n    94\t| Option | Purpose |\n    95\t|---|---|\n    96\t| `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description` | The chart's visible heading and accessible name (A1). `title` may carry a heading `level` and `visible: false`; `labelledBy` points at a heading the page already has. |\n    97\t| `state` | `'ready'`, `'loading'`, `{ error }`, `{ empty }` (L7). |\n    98\t| `legend`, `tooltip`, `table` | On by default; each can be turned off. |\n    99\t| `zoom`, `reference` | Cartesian charts only. |\n   100\t| `compact` | Line, area and bar: marks only, as a static image with a text alternative (spec 3.7). |\n   101\t| `colors` | A fixed color per series name (DX6). |\n   102\t| `messages`, `locale` | Built-in text and formatting locale (A10, F6). |\n   103\t| `strict`, `onDiagnostic` | Diagnostics delivery (section 3.5 of the spec). |\n   104\t\n   105\tThere is no `width`, `height`, `responsive`, `theme`, `palette` or `animation` option. Size comes from the container, colors from CSS, motion from the spec (I5). An unknown key raises AX150 with the closest known name.\n   106\t\n   107\t### 1.5 Handle and commit contract\n   108\t\n   109\t```ts\n   110\tchart.update({ title: 'Sessions' });                 // options\n   111\tchart.append([{ day: '2026-05-01', channel: 'Direct', sessions: 41210 }], { keep: 120 });   // rows\n   112\tchart.zoom(['2026-03-01', '2026-03-31']);            // view\n   113\tconst off = chart.on('hover', point => { … });\n   114\tchart.diagnostics;                                   // current, as soon as any call returns\n   115\tchart.destroy();\n   116\t```\n   117\t\n   118\tFinding D3: the ten libraries have seven update idioms. Axylo has three methods, one per kind of change, with one contract.\n   119\t\n   120\t| Rule | Detail |\n   121\t|---|---|\n   122\t| Order | Calls are applied in the order made. `update({ data })` followed by `append` appends to the new data. |\n   123\t| Two phases | Each call validates and normalizes synchronously: diagnostics are current when it returns, and a `strict` chart throws from that call. Drawing is deferred to the next animation frame, and calls made before that frame are drawn once. |\n   124\t| Atomic switch | Marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer move to a new data revision together. No frame mixes two revisions. |\n   125\t| Patches | A key present in an `update` replaces the old value whole; a key set to `undefined` returns to its default; absent keys stay. No deep merging. |\n   126\t| Rejected changes | A call that throws under `strict` leaves the chart on its last valid state. |\n   127\t| Streaming | `keep` is the number of distinct x values retained. Rows before the last x are accepted through the slower full update and raise AX151. While zoomed to the latest edge the view follows new data; zoomed elsewhere, it stays put. Paused charts buffer (I6). Keyboard focus stays on its datum until that datum leaves the window (A7). |\n   128\t| Teardown | `destroy()` cancels the pending frame, disconnects observers and listeners, and makes later calls no-ops. |\n   129\t\n   130\t### 1.6 Diagnostics\n   131\t\n   132\tThe registry, with each code's level and outcome, is in the spec (section 3.5). Example for the Phase 1 combined dirty fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`):\n   133\t\n   134\t```\n   135\t[axylo AX103] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" has 1 value that is not a finite number (NaN at row 3). Shown as a gap.\n   136\t  Check the calculation that produced it, or send null for \"no data\".\n   137\t[axylo AX106] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" mixes numbers with 1 string (\"40\" at row 4). The string is not plotted.\n   138\t  Convert it to a number where the data is produced.\n   139\t[axylo AX102] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" is absent in 1 row (row 5). Shown as a gap.\n   140\t  Send null if the value is known to be missing.\n   141\t```\n   142\t\n   143\tThe reader of that chart sees \"Values not shown: 3. See table.\" (F12).\n   144\t\n   145\t`validate(type, options)` runs the same normalization without a DOM and returns the diagnostics, so a server, a test or a coding agent can check a chart before rendering it.\n   146\t\n   147\tDevelopment and production flavours are selected through package `exports` conditions. The script-tag distribution ships `axylo.js` (development) and `axylo.min.js` (production). Errors and warnings print once per code per page in both; hints only in development.\n   148\t\n   149\t### 1.7 React adapter\n   150\t\n   151\t```tsx\n   152\timport { Line } from '@axylo/react';\n   153\t\n   154\t<Line data={rows} x=\"day\" y=\"sessions\" series=\"channel\" title=\"Daily sessions by channel\" />\n   155\t```\n   156\t\n   157\t| Behavior | Detail |\n   158\t|---|---|\n   159\t| Lifecycle | Create on mount, `destroy` on unmount; correct under StrictMode's setup, cleanup, setup. The file is marked `'use client'`. |\n   160\t| Props to options | Field specs are compared structurally, so an inline `x={{ field: 'ts' }}` does not renormalize on every render. `data` is compared by identity; in development, the same content arriving three times with a new identity raises a hint to memoize it. |\n   161\t| Removed props | A prop that disappears is sent as `undefined`, which restores the default. |\n   162\t| Handlers | Replaced without recreating the chart. |\n   163\t| Streaming | A ref exposes the handle, so `append`, `zoom` and `diagnostics` are reachable. When `data` is controlled, a new `data` prop replaces whatever was appended. |\n   164\t\n   165\tThe adapter is prototyped in M3, before the API freezes, because it is the cheapest way to find out whether the core contract survives a second consumer. It ships in 1.0 because finding 1.1 of the landscape shows where everyday charts are written; that is evidence of where charts are written, not of demand for Axylo.\n   166\t\n   167\t## 2. Architecture\n   168\t\n   169\t### 2.1 Pipeline\n   170\t\n   171\t```\n   172\toptions ─► normalize ─► frame ──┬─► view model ─► layout ─► scene ─► mark renderer\n   173\t            │ validate   (authoritative) │   scales, visible range         text renderer (DOM)\n   174\t            ▼ diagnostics                ├─► lookup: exact values by x, by point, by ordinal\n   175\t                                         ├─► summary and table\n   176\t                                         └─► focus model\n   177\t```\n   178\t\n   179\tThe **frame** is the normalized, lossless data: typed columns, a validity bitmap, dictionary-encoded categories, source-row indexes and a revision number. It is the only source for exact values. The table, the summary, keyboard navigation, tooltips and events read the frame. The **scene** is geometry for drawing and may be simplified (F5); nothing reads values back from it. The first draft derived accessibility from the scene, and two reviewers showed that a simplified drawing cannot return the samples it dropped.\n   180\t\n   181\t| Stage | Output | Notes |\n   182\t|---|---|---|\n   183\t| normalize | Frame, diagnostics | The only place raw input is read. Never coerces silently. |\n   184\t| view model | Scales, visible index ranges, series visibility | Domain rules implement F1 and F7. |\n   185\t| lookup | Nearest-x by binary search; nearest-point by a grid index (scatter) | Works on the frame within the visible range; cost bounded independently of the drawing. |\n   186\t| layout | Plot rectangle, tick sets, label boxes, legend and control placement | Implements L1, L3, L8. Takes a text-measurement interface as input. |\n   187\t| scene | Flat list of primitives (`path`, `rect`, `arc`, `rule`, `text`) | Plain data; testable in Node with injected text metrics. |\n   188\t| renderers | Marks; DOM text; accessibility DOM | See 2.3. |\n   189\t\n   190\t### 2.2 Invalidation\n   191\t\n   192\tStages are cached, and the cache keys are explicit. Caching on object identity alone, as the first draft said, would miss font loads, pixel-ratio changes and in-place mutation.\n   193\t\n   194\t| Stage | Reruns when |\n   195\t|---|---|\n   196\t| normalize | Data revision or field encoding changes. `append` extends the frame without renormalizing earlier rows. |\n   197\t| view model | Frame revision, zoom, series visibility, author domains. |\n   198\t| layout | View model; container size; resolved font and text spacing; `document.fonts` finishing a load; locale; any label text. A data-only update reruns layout when the tick labels would change, and skips it otherwise. |\n   199\t| scene | Layout or view model. |\n   200\t| mark raster | Scene; device pixel ratio; resolved colors (L4's triggers). |\n   201\t\n   202\tText is measured with the page's computed font, letter spacing and word spacing applied to the measuring context, and layout reruns when fonts finish loading. The measurement interface is a parameter, so Node tests inject fixed metrics and browser tests use the real one.\n   203\t\n   204\t### 2.3 Rendering\n   205\t\n   206\t**Working assumption: Canvas 2D for marks, HTML for all text.** Text stays in the DOM so that it inherits the page font and color, honors user text spacing and zoom (A6), adapts in forced-colors mode, and can be selected and translated.\n   207\t\n   208\t**This is reopened and decided in M1 by measurement.** The first draft justified canvas by Phase 1's slow SVG libraries, but those create a node per point; the one-path-per-series SVG chart in the harness was fast. With drawing simplified to a few vertices per pixel column (F5), an SVG path per series is small. SVG follows CSS variables and class-toggled themes with no repaint code, adapts to forced colors, prints sharply, needs no pixel-ratio handling and allocates no backing store (a 1,920×1,080 canvas at pixel ratio 2 is about 33 MB). Canvas is likely cheaper for per-frame redraw when streaming and hovering.\n   209\t\n   210\tM1 builds both mark renderers behind the same scene and compares them on: P1, P2, P3, P5, bytes, memory, a runtime theme toggle, forced colors, print, and browser zoom. The scene is renderer-neutral either way, so the choice is contained.\n   211\t\n   212\t**Simplified drawing.** Per device-pixel column and per contiguous run of valid samples, the stroke covers the run's minimum and maximum (F5). One rule for when it applies: more than four visible samples per device-pixel column on average. The pass over visible samples is O(visible) on typed columns and is measured separately from drawing; the vertex count is bounded by width times the number of runs, not by width alone.\n   213\t\n   214\t**No animation system.** First render is not animated (I5). Updates cut, or tween for at most 200 ms where the cost is negligible, and not at all under reduced motion.\n   215\t\n   216\t### 2.4 Document structure\n   217\t\n   218\t```html\n   219\t<figure class=\"axylo\" id=\"ax7\" role=\"group\" aria-labelledby=\"ax7-t\" aria-describedby=\"ax7-s\">\n   220\t  <figcaption>\n   221\t    <h3 id=\"ax7-t\">Daily sessions by channel</h3>        <!-- level from options; or omitted when labelledBy is used -->\n   222\t    <p id=\"ax7-s\" class=\"axylo-summary\">Line chart, 3 series, …</p>\n   223\t  </figcaption>\n   224\t  <div class=\"axylo-plot\" tabindex=\"0\"> … marks … <div class=\"axylo-text\" aria-hidden=\"true\">tick labels</div></div>\n   225\t  <div class=\"axylo-toolbar\" role=\"toolbar\" aria-label=\"Chart controls\">   <!-- one tab stop, roving focus -->\n   226\t    legend toggles · zoom in · zoom out · reset · pause · view as table\n   227\t  </div>\n   228\t  <p class=\"axylo-note\" role=\"status\">Values not shown: 2. See table.</p>\n   229\t  <div class=\"axylo-live\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div>\n   230\t  <div class=\"axylo-tooltip\" hidden></div>\n   231\t  <div class=\"axylo-table\" hidden><!-- built when opened: caption, headers, pager --></div>\n   232\t</figure>\n   233\t```\n   234\t\n   235\tIds are generated per chart. The plot's role, and how a focused point is conveyed, are not fixed here: M1 runs a spike with three candidate patterns and M4 confirms the choice (section 4).\n   236\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba docs/01-landscape.md | sed -n '95,111p;248,341p' && nl -ba docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md && nl -ba acceptance/README.md && nl -ba reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/dist.report.md | sed -n '31,85p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba docs/01-landscape.md | sed -n '95,111p;248,341p' && nl -ba docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md && nl -ba acceptance/README.md && nl -ba reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/dist.report.md | sed -n '31,85p'\"","aggregated_output":"    95\t### 1.4 Maintenance and cost facts\n    96\t\n    97\t| Library | Version tested | Published | Stable releases, 12 months | Major versions since Nov 2024 | License | Minified + gzipped, harness module | Harness lines |\n    98\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n    99\t| Chart.js | 4.6.1 | 2026-10-22 | 1 | none | MIT | 81 KiB | 54 |\n   100\t| Recharts | 3.11.0 | 2026-11-17 | 12 | v3 (2025-06) | MIT | 140 KiB + React | 83 + 9 shared |\n   101\t| D3 | 7.9.2 | 2026-11-03 | 2 | none | ISC | 35 KiB | 91 |\n   102\t| ECharts | 6.1.0 | 2026-05-19 | 1 | v6 (2025-07) | Apache-2.0 | 373 KiB | 63 |\n   103\t| Highcharts | 13.1.1 | 2026-09-20 | 10 | v12 (2024-11), v13 (2026-06) | Commercial | 143 KiB, accessibility module included | 51 |\n   104\t| ApexCharts | 8.9.0 | 2026-11-17 | 65 | v5 (2025-07), v6 (2026-07), v7 (2026-08), v8 (2026-10) | Free below a size threshold, otherwise commercial | 289 KiB | 60 |\n   105\t| Plotly.js | 4.3.0 | 2026-11-10 | 12 | v3 (2025-01), v4 (2026-08) | MIT | 1,450 KiB | 30 |\n   106\t| Nivo | 0.99.0 | 2025-05-23 | 0 | none | MIT | 129 KiB + React | 54 + 9 shared |\n   107\t| MUI X Charts | 9.18.1 | 2026-11-16 | 43 | v8 (2025-04), v9 (2026-04) | MIT; zoom and pan need the Pro plan | 160 KiB + React | 44 + 9 shared |\n   108\t| uPlot | 1.6.32 | 2025-03-14 | 0 | none | MIT | 23 KiB, no donut | 45 |\n   109\t\n   110\t**Observed** from the npm registry and from bundling each harness module with esbuild. The sizes and line counts are from the first probe pass and are not like for like: each size is the whole harness module (line, bar, donut where it exists, and the load tasks) through the import path the library's getting-started guide shows, with CSS counted as text. Most libraries document a smaller modular import that was not measured. Release counts describe package history; they do not measure how hard an upgrade is. License terms are **Docs** and were read once: the Highcharts shop listed 366 USD per seat for Highcharts Core; ApexCharts' license file sets a $2M threshold and has separate terms for embedding charts in a product, with details on its licensing page that I did not verify; MUI's accessibility page marks zoom and pan as Pro.\n   111\t\n   248\t## 4. How readers explore and interact\n   249\t\n   250\t### 4.1 Scoreboard\n   251\t\n   252\tAll cells are **Observed** in Chrome's accessibility tree and by scripted keyboard and pointer input. No screen reader was run.\n   253\t\n   254\t| | Chart.js | Recharts | D3 (authored) | ECharts | Highcharts + module | ApexCharts | Plotly.js | Nivo | MUI X | uPlot |\n   255\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n   256\t| What the accessibility tree holds | nothing | legend list; an `application` element with no label, containing the tick text | image containing all the chart's text | nothing | named region, heading, generated description, a region per series | named `application`, legend buttons | two unnamed images, a toolbar | unnamed image | legend list, status region; the `title` label sits on a `role=\"none\"` element | title text, legend table |\n   257\t| Tab stops in the chart | 0 | 8, of which 7 land on unlabeled groups | 0 | 0 | 2 | 10 | 9: eight toolbar buttons and a link | 0 | 1 | 0 |\n   258\t| Arrow keys move between points | no | yes | no | no | yes | yes | no | no | yes | no |\n   259\t| Focused point's text reaches a live region | no | yes, `assertive` | no | no | no: tooltip only, the polite region stayed empty | yes, polite | no | no | no: tooltip only | no |\n   260\t| Focused point shown visually | – | not in the screenshot | – | – | yes | yes | – | – | yes | – |\n   261\t| Tooltip with the pointer between points | none | all series | all series (authored) | all series | one series | all series | one point | one point | all series | all series, in the legend |\n   262\t| Follows a container resize without code | yes | yes | scales like an image | **no** | yes | yes | **no** | yes | yes | **no** |\n   263\t| Bars animate on first render | yes | yes | no | yes | yes | yes | no | yes | yes | no |\n   264\t| …and stop under reduced motion | **no** | yes | – | **no** | **no** | yes | – | **no** | yes | – |\n   265\t\n   266\t![After Tab and four keys](../research/harness/shots/_sheet-kbd.png)\n   267\t\n   268\t### 4.2 Findings\n   269\t\n   270\t**R11. Half of the ten give a keyboard or assistive-technology user nothing to work with by default.** Chart.js and ECharts put one unlabeled canvas on the page. uPlot adds a title and a legend table that reads `--` until a mouse moves. Nivo is an unnamed image; the authored D3 chart is an image whose only content is its tick text. Chart.js' documentation leaves making the canvas accessible to the author (**Docs**, page dated 2021).\n   271\t\n   272\t**R12. Four configurations have keyboard navigation, with rough edges.** It was on by default in Recharts, ApexCharts and MUI X (the ApexCharts and MUI X documentation confirm the default), and in Highcharts once its module is imported. Observed rough edges:\n   273\t\n   274\t- Recharts and ApexCharts use `role=\"application\"`, which changes how screen readers pass keys through. Recharts' element has no label of its own. Leaving the Recharts chart took eight Tab presses.\n   275\t- ApexCharts has ten tab stops on this chart, six of them toolbar buttons for a zoomable time axis. **Inferred**: a dashboard of such charts multiplies that.\n   276\t- Highcharts and MUI X moved a visual focus marker and tooltip, but in this probe the point's text never reached a live region or the focused element's name. Whether a screen reader conveys it another way was not tested.\n   277\t- MUI X's documentation says the feature is still being developed (**Docs**).\n   278\t- Plotly's tab stops are its toolbar; the data cannot be reached.\n   279\t\n   280\t**R13. One configuration describes the chart.** Highcharts with its module generated: \"Line chart with 3 lines. The chart has 1 X axis displaying Time. Data ranges from 2026-01-01 00:00:00 to 2026-04-30 00:00:00. The chart has 1 Y axis displaying Sessions. Data ranges from 2732 to 48085.\" No tested configuration offers a data table without a further module.\n   281\t\n   282\t**R14. The bar entry animation ignores reduced motion in four of the seven that animate.** Chart.js, ECharts, Highcharts and Nivo produced the same early-frame difference with `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` as without. None of their packages contains that media query string ([`spot-checks.json`](../research/harness/spot-checks.json)). Recharts, ApexCharts and MUI X stop.\n   283\t\n   284\t**R15. Tooltips differ in basic behavior.** With the pointer in the plot between points, Chart.js shows nothing; its default requires the pointer to intersect a point. Highcharts, Plotly and Nivo describe one series or point; the others list all series at that x. MUI X's tooltip extended past the container's right edge.\n   285\t\n   286\t**R16. Three libraries do not follow their container.** ECharts, Plotly and uPlot stayed 720 px wide when the container shrank to 360 px. Each documents a resize call or option. uPlot needs pixel sizes up front.\n   287\t\n   288\t![Created at 340 px](../research/harness/shots/_sheet-narrow.png)\n   289\t\n   290\t### 4.3 Accessibility with each library's switch off and on\n   291\t\n   292\tThe first version compared Highcharts with its module against nine libraries without an equivalent. Both states, where a single documented switch exists:\n   293\t\n   294\t| Library | Switch | Off | On |\n   295\t|---|---|---|---|\n   296\t| Highcharts | Import `modules/accessibility` | One image named by the title; 0 tab stops; a console message recommends the module | Named region, generated description, keyboard navigation, 2 tab stops |\n   297\t| ECharts | `aria: { enabled: true }` (no extra import with the full build) | One unlabeled canvas | `role=\"img\"` with a generated label that lists the first ten items of each series, timestamps as raw numbers; still 0 tab stops |\n   298\t| MUI X Charts | `title` prop | Legend list and status region; no named chart | The label is set on an element with `role=\"none\"`, so no named chart appeared in the tree |\n   299\t| Recharts, ApexCharts | On by default | – | As in 4.1 |\n   300\t| Chart.js, Plotly.js, Nivo, uPlot, D3 | None found | As in 4.1 | – |\n   301\t\n   302\tSo \"Highcharts is the strongest\" holds only with its module, which is one import. By default it is in the weaker half.\n   303\t\n   304\t## 5. How developers create, update and maintain\n   305\t\n   306\tCustomization was not compared (section 2).\n   307\t\n   308\t### 5.1 What each library needed beyond data\n   309\t\n   310\t**Observed** while writing the harness ([`research/harness/src/libs/`](../research/harness/src/libs)). These are what one author needed, not proof that no shorter way exists.\n   311\t\n   312\t| Library | Had to know or add |\n   313\t|---|---|\n   314\t| Chart.js | A date adapter package before a time axis works. `maintainAspectRatio: false` to fill a fixed-height container. |\n   315\t| Recharts | Reshape the data into row objects. A color for every series. A tick formatter for a numeric time axis. Eight components for one line chart. |\n   316\t| D3 | Everything: title, legend, readout and axes are authored, with no keyboard access. |\n   317\t| ECharts | `tooltip` and `legend` are absent unless declared. |\n   318\t| Highcharts | The accessibility module is a separate import. |\n   319\t| ApexCharts | A numeric height: `height: '100%'` produced a 456 px chart in a 400 px container. |\n   320\t| Plotly.js | Nothing extra. The shortest module, and 1,450 KiB gzipped with the documented import. |\n   321\t| Nivo | Margins by hand, or axes are clipped. `useMesh` for a tooltip. A five-property object for a legend. A canvas line component exists and was not tested. |\n   322\t| MUI X Charts | `@mui/material` and `@mui/system` as peer dependencies. It also accepts row objects through a `dataset` prop, which the harness did not use. |\n   323\t| uPlot | Pixel width and height, a stroke color per series, x in seconds (or an option to accept milliseconds), a path builder plus a custom axis for bars. |\n   324\t\n   325\t### 5.2 Findings\n   326\t\n   327\t**D1. Line counts are similar; hidden knowledge is not.** What separates the libraries is the list above: things that give a broken or blank chart with no message when missed.\n   328\t\n   329\t**D2. Five data shapes for the same table.** Parallel arrays with labels (Chart.js, uPlot), `[x, y]` pairs per series (ECharts, Highcharts, ApexCharts), row objects keyed by series (Recharts), `{ id, data: [{ x, y }] }` (Nivo), and separate x and y arrays (Plotly, MUI X). The harness fixture is columnar and had to be reshaped a different way for each.\n   330\t\n   331\t**D3. Seven update idioms.** Mutate then `chart.update()` (Chart.js); `setOption` (ECharts); `series.addPoint` (Highcharts); `updateSeries` (ApexCharts); `Plotly.extendTraces`; `setData` (uPlot); re-render with new props (the React three).\n   332\t\n   333\t**D4. Bundle cost spans a wide range** with the documented imports: 23 KiB to 1,450 KiB gzipped for the harness modules (section 1.4, with its caveats).\n   334\t\n   335\t**D5. Errors do not help.** The one exception in the study came from minified code and named neither the option nor the data.\n   336\t\n   337\t**D6. Release cadence ranges from none to very frequent.** Nivo and uPlot have had no release in 18 and 20 months. ApexCharts shipped four major versions in 16 months. Whether either is a problem for a user depends on what broke, which this study did not test.\n   338\t\n   339\t**D7. Licensing splits the field.** Highcharts is paid for commercial use; ApexCharts is free only below a size threshold; MUI X charges for zoom and pan. The fully permissive libraries include the ones with the weakest accessibility defaults, plus Recharts and MUI X's free tier.\n   340\t\n   341\t### 5.3 Load\n     1\t# Decision 0002: SVG paths draw the marks by default (provisional)\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: **provisional working default**, set by the coordinator on 2026-11-18 from the M1 measurements and weakened the same day after an independent review of the benchmark harness (`reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/bench.review.md`, Codex gpt-6.2-sol at effort max). The owner asked for this to be settled by measurement. It is not settled: the measurements support using SVG while the line-chart slice is built and keeping canvas behind the internal switch. They do not establish a shipping winner or a reason to remove either renderer.\n     4\t\n     5\t## What the review changed\n     6\t\n     7\tThe table below is kept as measured. These statements made from it in the first version are withdrawn or narrowed:\n     8\t\n     9\t- **Withdrawn: \"nothing measured favours canvas except all-thread CPU during resize.\"** The recorded heap, DOM-node and layout counts also favour canvas.\n    10\t- **Withdrawn: the resize CPU gap is raster work.** The number is CPU for the whole renderer process; no stage was traced.\n    11\t- **Withdrawn: simplification bounds every chart by its width.** It was shown for line series only; point marks and many-series charts were not measured.\n    12\t- **Withdrawn: an SVG theme change needs no run-time work.** Both renderers recorded six DOM mutations per toggle; \"no canvas drawing call\" is all that was observed.\n    13\t- **Withdrawn: normalization and garbage collection explain the gap to uPlot.** About 71–73% of busy time in the production profiles is unmapped; the normalization share comes from development builds. It is a hypothesis to test, not a finding.\n    14\t- **Narrowed: \"half the renderer memory.\"** The halving is of the increase above a data-only page at pixel ratio 2 (34.2 → 17.7 MiB). Absolute renderer memory was 74.7 → 56.2 MiB, and at pixel ratio 1 the increase was 23.1 → 18.4 MiB.\n    15\t- **Narrowed: \"no frame later than 25 ms.\"** True of the recorded animation-frame callbacks; the window can miss an interval that spans its boundary, and a callback is not a presented frame.\n    16\t- **Void: every 24-chart page row** (page at pixel ratio 1 and 2, resize of the 24-chart page, and the memory row). The harness fix pass found that these runs drew no marks: at 230 × 115 px each tile is below the 120 × 80 px plot minimum of spec L8, so every chart showed its text fallback. The SVG records carry `svgPaths: 0`; the canvas runs held empty canvases. The numbers compare two empty charts and say nothing about the renderers. The harness now rejects a run whose chart drew no marks, and the page workload uses 340 × 200 px tiles.\n    17\t- **Narrowed: verdicts.** 149 metric comparisons were judged without adjustment for choosing among them, on six pairs or fewer per workload, with noise bands from one session and two workload shapes. Two \"SVG lower\" rows allow a gain as small as 0.16 ms (10,000 points) and 6.3 ms (dashboard at pixel ratio 2).\n    18\t\n    19\tEvidence: [bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md](../../bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md). One session of 39 runs, production build, headless Chrome 157, machine under load from other work, A-versus-A noise bands for two workloads only.\n    20\t\n    21\t## What was measured\n    22\t\n    23\tCanvas → SVG, medians of main-thread task time unless stated. \"Lower\" means the 95% interval cleared zero and the noise band where one existed.\n    24\t\n    25\t| Workload | Canvas | SVG | Verdict |\n    26\t|---|---|---|---|\n    27\t| Streaming, per update | 2.26 ms | 1.45 ms | SVG lower |\n    28\t| Theme change, per toggle | 2.68 ms and 27 drawing calls | 2.01 ms and no drawing call | SVG lower |\n    29\t| Resize, one chart of 100,000 points | 915 ms | 472 ms | SVG lower on the main thread |\n    30\t| Same, CPU across all threads | 1,035 ms | 1,770 ms | **SVG higher** |\n    31\t| Resize, 24-chart page | 730 ms | 525 ms | Void: no marks were drawn |\n    32\t| First chart, 10,000 points | 34.0 ms | 28.4 ms | SVG lower |\n    33\t| 24-chart page at pixel ratio 2 | 199 ms | 158 ms | Void: no marks were drawn |\n    34\t| First chart, 3 × 120 | 23.6 ms | 19.8 ms | Inconclusive |\n    35\t| First chart, 100,000 points | 81.8 ms | 83.5 ms | Inconclusive |\n    36\t| First chart, 1,000,000 points | 622 ms | 652 ms | Inconclusive (4 blocks) |\n    37\t| Memory, 24-chart page at pixel ratio 2 | 34.2 MiB renderer, 11.5 MiB GPU, 7.7 MiB backing stores | 17.7 MiB, 2.2 MiB, none | Void: no marks were drawn |\n    38\t\n    39\tNo frame was later than 25 ms in any configuration, so frame pacing does not separate the two.\n    40\t\n    41\t## Why SVG for now\n    42\t\n    43\t1. On the line workloads measured, main-thread task time was lower with SVG or the two could not be told apart.\n    44\t2. Line series are simplified to a few vertices per pixel column before either renderer sees them, so for line charts the path size follows the plot width, not the data.\n    45\t3. SVG follows the page with less code: forced colors and print work through CSS, browser zoom stays sharp, and there is no backing store to size, cap or lose. This is a design argument, not a measurement.\n    46\t\n    47\t## What this does not establish\n    48\t\n    49\t- **GPU rasterization was not measured.** Headless Chrome here does not use a GPU. The resize, pixel-ratio-2 and process-memory results need a headed, GPU-backed rerun before they transfer.\n    50\t- **Dense point marks were not measured.** A scatter chart with tens of thousands of markers is the case most likely to favour canvas.\n    51\t- **One browser, one machine, one session.** Firefox and Safari may order the two differently.\n    52\t- **Many series, pan and zoom, and the budget workloads P4, P7 and P8 were not measured**, and the million-point comparison has four pairs.\n    53\t- Both renderers are far from uPlot in the configurations measured (about 80 ms against 12 ms at 100,000 points; uPlot draws no tooltip or accessibility layer). Where that time goes is not established.\n    54\t\n    55\t## Consequences\n    56\t\n    57\t- The working default is SVG. The canvas renderer stays in the code behind the same internal switch, with the pixel-agreement tests between the two kept.\n    58\t- Before the choice is final: fix the harness defects the review found (renderer identity in the readback, noise bands on replay, failed runs excluded from speed verdicts, gap-aware admission, the frame window), declare the primary endpoints and thresholds in advance, then rerun on a quiet machine with scatter at 10,000, 50,000 and 200,000 points, ten series of 100,000 points, pan and zoom, and the byte cost of keeping both renderers. Removing canvas from the bundle needs that evidence too.\n    59\t- When a real GPU and a second browser are available: rerun the resize and million-point workloads headed. If canvas wins there by more than the shipping thresholds in the performance plan, flip the switch back.\n    60\t- The plan's sections 2.2 and 2.3 are read with SVG in the \"marks\" position: a theme change no longer needs a redraw, and device pixel ratio matters only to simplification.\n     1\t# Release acceptance\n     2\t\n     3\tFrom the repository root\n     4\t\n     5\t```sh\n     6\tnode --test acceptance/test/*.test.mjs\n     7\tnode acceptance/check.mjs\n     8\tmkdir -p acceptance/results\n     9\tenv -u AXYLO_BROWSER timeout 120 npx vitest run --reporter=json --outputFile=acceptance/results/unit.json\n    10\tnode acceptance/check.mjs --results acceptance/results/unit.json\n    11\tnode acceptance/check.mjs --release --results acceptance/results/unit.json\n    12\t```\n    13\t\n    14\tSupply any number of Vitest JSON reports after `--results`, including browser reports collected on a machine that can run them. The checker prints the table and rewrites [report.md](report.md). Structural errors always exit 1. With `--release`, any `fail` or `no evidence` also exits 1. Review the other open statuses before tagging a release.\n    15\t\n    16\t| Status | Meaning |\n    17\t|---|---|\n    18\t| pass | Every required test has a passing assertion result. |\n    19\t| fail | At least one required test failed in a supplied report. |\n    20\t| no evidence | The criterion applies and has no qualifying evidence. Weak checks appear in the gap list. |\n    21\t| not run | Required results are absent or skipped, or a provisional budget has no matching measurement. |\n    22\t| cannot be verified here | A required environment or rehearsal is unavailable, or an archived measurement has no frozen limit to compare against. The report links measured history without a verdict. |\n    23\t| not applicable | The criterion does not apply to this type. The cell gives the reason. |\n    24\t\n    25\tFirefox, Safari/WebKit, native phone behavior, screen-reader rehearsal and hardware GPU rasterisation cannot be verified here. The cited benchmark records use software rasterisation and measure main-thread work. Hardware rasterisation has no pass/fail budget of its own in spec §5.\n    26\t\n    27\t## Add a row\n    28\t\n    29\tUse the ID and short wording from the spec. Section 3.6 cells use `T-<rule>-<chart type>`. Add an `appliesTo` list and give a `notApplicable` reason for every excluded type. Negative type-matrix cells such as “No” zoom are applicable checks of absence. The compact column covers the variant defined in §3.7.\n    30\t\n    31\tRead each test body and file it under the chart type it actually exercises. An `evidence` item takes a repository-relative `file` and a `kind` of `unit`, `property`, `browser`, `benchmark`, `document` or `manual rehearsal`. For tests use the exact Vitest `title` or a `prefix` of a `describe` title when the entire suite is needed. Add a `reason` to each `weak` item. Benchmark entries take a `workload` and optional `history` paths. Use `cannotVerify` for environmental limits and `pending` for provisional work, with reasons by type.\n    32\t\n    33\tThe source scanner checks conventional registrations and simple parameterized titles without importing tests. Its limits are documented in [titles.mjs](titles.mjs). A failed assertion takes precedence across reports. Documents, manual notes and provisional benchmark measurements require review outside the Vitest result join.\n    31\t### What ran (all observed)\n    32\t- Node, `npx vitest run --config tests/dist/vitest.config.ts`: `Test Files 8 passed (8) / Tests 83 passed (83)`.\n    33\t- Browser, `AXYLO_BROWSER=1 npx vitest run --config tests/dist/vitest.config.ts`: `Test Files 2 passed (2) / Tests 18 passed (18)`.\n    34\t- `npx tsc -p tests/dist`: exit 0.\n    35\t- `packages/react/test/dist.test.ts`: still 12/12 after the metadata changes.\n    36\t\n    37\tWhat each group shows:\n    38\t- **Isolation:** the fixture packages are real directories, not links. Node (with and without `--conditions=development`) and esbuild resolve `axylo`, `axylo/axylo.css`, `@axylo/react`, and the adapter's own `import 'axylo'`, to fixture paths only. A control assertion confirms the root `node_modules/axylo` really does link to the workspace, so the check has something to catch.\n    39\t- **Package contents:** every `exports` target under every condition, and every `files` entry, is in the tarball. Nothing else ships beyond the declarations the types entries import, the two IIFE files, README, LICENSE and package.json. No source, tests or source maps; declarations import only peer dependencies.\n    40\t- **Types:** strict `tsc` with `skipLibCheck: false` passes on a consumer file and on each snippet, in both fixtures. `--listFiles` shows the declarations came from the fixture. The `@ts-expect-error` lines for the DX3 mistakes behave correctly, so the declarations are real types, not `any`.\n    41\t- **Conditions:** `development` and the default are different files in Node and in esbuild, and neither contains `__DEV__`.\n    42\t  - How I checked for development-only code: I found every `if (__DEV__) …` in the source and bundled the source twice, once as is and once with those statements cut out. Text present only in the first bundle counts as development-only.\n    43\t  - Found for `axylo`: `__dev`, `stages`, `retainedRows`. For `@axylo/react`: `[axylo/react] <`, `> was given `, `columns`.\n    44\t  - Each is present in the packed development file and absent from the production one.\n    45\t  - In the browser, the AX104 hint prints once in the development flavour and not at all in production.\n    46\t- **Side effects:** importing either package and using nothing bundles to 0 bytes. Importing `axylo` in Node adds no globals, and in Chrome it leaves the document untouched. `import 'axylo/axylo.css'` survives bundling.\n    47\t- **DX8 in Node:**\n    48\t  - Importing `axylo` works without DOM globals.\n    49\t  - `validate()` on the Phase 1 dirty fixture returns exactly AX102 (row 5), AX103 (row 3) and AX106 (row 4), as spec 3.5 and plan 1.6 say.\n    50\t  - `line('#nowhere')` with no document throws a message naming `#nowhere`.\n    51\t  - Server rendering of the snippet and all five components gives an empty `<div>` container each.\n    52\t- **DX1 in Chrome:**\n    53\t  - The npm snippet (production and development conditions), the script-tag snippet and the React snippet under StrictMode each draw one figure named \"Monthly visitors\" with series-painted marks, and print no console error or warning.\n    54\t  - Hydrating the server markup produces no mismatch.\n    55\t  - Without `title`, the chart still draws and raises only AX140.\n    56\t- **DX8 under CSP:** 8 page variants: {IIFE min, IIFE dev, bundled module prod, bundled module dev} × {stylesheet applied at run time, stylesheet linked by the page}. Each draws all five chart types plus the compact variant and opens tooltips and all five tables. Result: zero violation events, zero reports, zero console errors, no elements created from `<img onerror>` or `<script>` text in titles, series, categories, labels, units or formatters, and `#no-such-chart` is named in the thrown error. Run-time styling adopts exactly one sheet; the linked case adopts none; there are never any `<style>` elements. A negative control (an `insertAdjacentHTML` page) is caught by both instruments.\n    57\t  - The exact policy: `default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; report-uri /csp-report`\n    58\t- **DX11:** MIT field and LICENSE text in both packages. No runtime dependencies. The shipped JS imports nothing, except `react` and `axylo` from the adapter. Bundling each package's source with the release settings reads only its own `src`, so no `node_modules` code is compiled in. No licence headers in `packages/*/src` or in the shipped files.\n    59\t\n    60\t### Sizes (`node scripts/size.mjs`, from the packed files, esbuild minify, production condition, gzip level 9)\n    61\t\n    62\t| entry | min B | js gzip B | css file gzip B | total KiB | target KiB | status |\n    63\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    64\t| line only (`npm.ts`) | 282,947 | 99,956 | — | 97.61 | 25 | OVER |\n    65\t| everything | 283,389 | 100,093 | — | 97.75 | 40 | OVER |\n    66\t| script tag (IIFE min + css) | 283,869 | 100,179 | 4,095 | 101.83 | — | no target |\n    67\t| React adapter alone | 2,375 | 1,197 | — | 1.17 | 2 | ok |\n    68\t\n    69\t- `--check` exits 1.\n    70\t- The JS already carries the stylesheet string, so the bundler rows do not add the CSS file. Adding it, as the old script did, would add 4,095 B.\n    71\t- Breakdown by directory (minified bytes): data 24.3%, core 16.1%, dom 13.5%, theme 8.8%. Largest modules: `core/controller.ts`, `dom/interaction.ts`, `data/store.ts`, `theme/css.ts`.\n    72\t- A rebuild from source with the release settings is byte-identical in size to the packed `axylo.js`.\n    73\t- **Finding: chart types do not tree-shake.** Line-only saves 137 B gzip compared with everything. Building from source gives the same result (line-only 99,735 B, everything 99,912 B, `validate` only 20,686 B).\n    74\t  - The likely cause (inferred, not proven) is that every chart function goes through `createChart(type, …)` in `packages/axylo/src/core/chart.ts` / `core/controller.ts`, which reaches all types.\n    75\t\n    76\t### Failing tests\n    77\tNone among the 101. The only failure is `size.mjs --check`, above, for DX7.\n    78\t\n    79\t### Where the spec was ambiguous, and what I assumed\n    80\t1. **DX1 options:** \"no option besides data and fields\" conflicts with AX140, which is a warning (spec 3.5), and with \"no console warnings\". The snippets therefore pass `title`, as the plan 1.1 example does. A test shows the title is not required. `acceptance/matrix.json` DX1 still says \"only data and fields\".\n    81\t2. **Script-tag file names:** plan 1.6 calls the script-tag files `axylo.js`/`axylo.min.js`. The build produces `axylo.iife.js`/`axylo.iife.min.js`, and `axylo.js` is the production ESM file. The snippet uses `dist/axylo.iife.min.js`.\n    82\t3. **Script-tag URL:** the snippet uses unversioned `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/axylo/dist/...` for a package not yet published. The test routes those URLs to the packed files.\n    83\t4. **Script-tag target:** the spec gives no size target for the script-tag row.\n    84\t5. **React snippet shape:** it exports a component, which the fixture's `main.tsx` mounts.\n    85\t6. **Server markup:** the expected SSR output (an empty container) is taken from plan 1.7, \"create on mount\".\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba AGENTS.md && nl -ba scripts/size.mjs && nl -ba scripts/build.mjs && nl -ba packages/axylo/package.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba AGENTS.md && nl -ba scripts/size.mjs && nl -ba scripts/build.mjs && nl -ba packages/axylo/package.json'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Working in this repository\n     2\t\n     3\tAxylo is a browser charting library. The product is specified in `docs/02-product-spec.md`; the build plan is `docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md`. Read the sections your task names before writing code.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Rules for every change\n     6\t\n     7\t- **Contracts are fixed.** `packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts` and `packages/axylo/src/public.ts` are the interfaces between modules. Do not edit them. If your work needs a change there, stop that part, finish what you can, and describe the exact change you need in your report.\n     8\t- **Stay in your files.** Each task lists the directories it owns. Do not create, edit or delete anything else, including configuration and other modules. Several agents work in this checkout at the same time.\n     9\t- **No runtime dependencies.** Use the platform (`Intl`, typed arrays). Dev dependencies already installed: TypeScript, Vitest, fast-check, esbuild, Playwright (system Chrome), axe-core. The network is not available; do not try to install anything.\n    10\t- **No DOM at import time.** A module that needs the DOM takes it as a parameter or touches it inside a function. Pure stages (data, scales, formatting, layout, scene) must run in Node.\n    11\t- **Hot paths use typed arrays.** No object per data point, no array of arrays per data point, no copying of large columns that the contracts say are borrowed.\n    12\t- **Never guess silently.** Anything that cannot be drawn as given is reported through the `DiagnosticSink` with the code from spec section 3.5.\n    13\t- **Tests come from the spec.** Expected values are derived from the specification and the fixture data, never by running the implementation and pasting its output. Use fast-check property tests for invariants (\"for any data …\"). A test you cannot make pass is reported as failing; do not weaken it or change its expected value.\n    14\t- **TypeScript strict, no `any`** in exported signatures. `__DEV__` is a build-time boolean (true in tests).\n    15\t- **Comments say why**, not what. Names are plain words.\n    16\t- **Do not commit.** The coordinator commits.\n    17\t\n    18\t## Commands\n    19\t\n    20\t```bash\n    21\tnpm run typecheck      # tsc over the library, its tests, and the API examples in docs/api\n    22\tnpm test               # unit and property tests (Node)\n    23\tnpm run build          # packages/axylo/dist\n    24\tnpm run test:browser   # browser tests against the built bundle, in system Chrome\n    25\t```\n    26\t\n    27\t## Your report\n    28\t\n    29\tEnd with a short report: what you built (files), what you verified and how (paste the last lines of the test run), what is not done or not verified, any contract change you need, and any place where the spec was ambiguous and what you assumed. Keep \"ran and saw\" apart from \"expect\".\n     1\t// What a consumer downloads, minified and gzipped, measured from the packed tarballs (spec DX7, P6; plan M7).\n     2\t//\n     3\t//   node scripts/size.mjs           pack packages/*/dist (scripts/pack.mjs --no-build), measure, print, write bench/size-latest.json\n     4\t//   node scripts/size.mjs --check   the same, and exit 1 when a row exceeds its target\n     5\t//   node scripts/size.mjs --no-pack measure the fixtures already in tests/dist/fixtures\n     6\t//\n     7\t// Every row is built the way a consumer's bundler would build it: esbuild, from inside a fixture that installed\n     8\t// the tarball, minified, with the `production` export condition, so only the packed files and their `exports`\n     9\t// are read. The script-tag row is the two files the documented snippet loads, as shipped.\n    10\t//\n    11\t// Stylesheet: the JavaScript carries the stylesheet as a string and applies it at run time, so a bundler page\n    12\t// downloads it inside its JavaScript and needs no axylo.css; the script-tag snippet also links axylo.css, so\n    13\t// that row adds the file. \"total\" is what each page downloads. The targets are provisional (spec DX7).\n    14\t//\n    15\t// The per-module breakdown cannot come from the packed files, which are one pre-bundled file each: it rebuilds\n    16\t// packages/axylo/src with the release build's production settings (scripts/build.mjs) and reads esbuild's\n    17\t// metafile, then states how far that rebuild is from the packed axylo.js.\n    18\timport { build } from 'esbuild';\n    19\timport fs from 'node:fs';\n    20\timport path from 'node:path';\n    21\timport { gzipSync } from 'node:zlib';\n    22\timport { pack } from './pack.mjs';\n    23\t\n    24\tconst root = path.resolve(import.meta.dirname, '..');\n    25\tconst fixtures = path.join(root, 'tests/dist/fixtures');\n    26\tconst LEVEL = 9;\n    27\tconst gzip = bytes => gzipSync(bytes, { level: LEVEL }).length;\n    28\tconst kib = bytes => +(bytes / 1024).toFixed(2);\n    29\t\n    30\tif (!process.argv.includes('--no-pack')) pack({ build: false, log: () => {} });\n    31\tconst manifest = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.join(root, 'tests/dist/.packed/manifest.json'), 'utf8'));\n    32\t\n    33\tasync function consumer({ cwd, contents, external = [], metafile = false }) {\n    34\t  const r = await build({\n    35\t    stdin: { contents, resolveDir: cwd, loader: 'ts', sourcefile: 'consumer.ts' }, absWorkingDir: cwd, bundle: true, minify: true,\n    36\t    format: 'esm', target: 'es2022', conditions: ['production'], external, write: false, metafile, logLevel: 'silent',\n    37\t  });\n    38\t  const inputs = Object.keys(r.metafile?.inputs ?? {});\n    39\t  return { bytes: r.outputFiles[0].contents, inputs };\n    40\t}\n    41\t\n    42\tconst npm = path.join(fixtures, 'npm');\n    43\tconst react = path.join(fixtures, 'react');\n    44\tconst axyloFile = file => fs.readFileSync(path.join(npm, 'node_modules/axylo/dist', file));\n    45\tconst css = axyloFile('axylo.css');\n    46\t\n    47\tconst cases = [\n    48\t  {\n    49\t    entry: 'line only (examples/first-chart/npm.ts)', targetKiB: 25,\n    50\t    build: () => consumer({ cwd: npm, contents: fs.readFileSync(path.join(npm, 'example/npm.ts'), 'utf8') }), cssFile: false,\n    51\t  },\n    52\t  { entry: 'everything (export * from axylo)', targetKiB: 40, build: () => consumer({ cwd: npm, contents: \"export * from 'axylo';\" }), cssFile: false },\n    53\t  { entry: 'script tag (axylo.iife.min.js + axylo.css)', targetKiB: null, build: async () => ({ bytes: axyloFile('axylo.iife.min.js') }), cssFile: true },\n    54\t  {\n    55\t    entry: 'React adapter alone (react, axylo external)', targetKiB: 2, cssFile: false,\n    56\t    build: () => consumer({ cwd: react, contents: \"export * from '@axylo/react';\", external: ['react', 'react/*', 'react-dom', 'axylo'] }),\n    57\t  },\n    58\t];\n    59\t\n    60\tconst rows = [];\n    61\tfor (const c of cases) {\n    62\t  const { bytes } = await c.build();\n    63\t  const jsGzip = gzip(bytes);\n    64\t  const cssGzip = c.cssFile ? gzip(css) : 0;\n    65\t  const total = jsGzip + cssGzip;\n    66\t  rows.push({\n    67\t    entry: c.entry, minBytes: bytes.length, jsGzipBytes: jsGzip, cssFileGzipBytes: cssGzip, totalGzipBytes: total,\n    68\t    totalKiB: kib(total), targetKiB: c.targetKiB, over: c.targetKiB !== null && kib(total) > c.targetKiB,\n    69\t  });\n    70\t}\n    71\t\n    72\tconsole.log(`Download size from the packed tarballs (${manifest.packages.axylo.tarball}, ${manifest.packages['@axylo/react'].tarball}, packed ${manifest.packedAt}); esbuild minify, gzip level ${LEVEL}`);\n    73\tconsole.table(rows.map(r => ({\n    74\t  entry: r.entry, 'min B': r.minBytes, 'js gzip B': r.jsGzipBytes, 'css file gzip B': r.cssFileGzipBytes || '—',\n    75\t  'total KiB': r.totalKiB, 'target KiB': r.targetKiB ?? '—', status: r.targetKiB === null ? 'no target' : r.over ? 'OVER' : 'ok',\n    76\t})));\n    77\tconsole.log(`The stylesheet is ${css.length} B, ${gzip(css)} B gzipped as a file; the same text is inside every JavaScript row above.`);\n    78\t\n    79\t// Where the bytes of \"everything\" are: the source rebuilt as scripts/build.mjs builds dist/axylo.js.\n    80\tconst src = path.join(root, 'packages/axylo/src');\n    81\tconst rebuilt = await build({\n    82\t  entryPoints: [path.join(src, 'index.ts')], bundle: true, target: 'es2022', legalComments: 'none', format: 'esm', minify: true,\n    83\t  define: { __DEV__: 'false' }, write: false, metafile: true, logLevel: 'silent', absWorkingDir: root,\n    84\t});\n    85\tconst packedProd = axyloFile('axylo.js').length;\n    86\tconst out = Object.values(rebuilt.metafile.outputs)[0];\n    87\tconst modules = Object.entries(out.inputs).map(([file, v]) => ({ file: path.relative(src, path.resolve(root, file)), bytes: v.bytesInOutput }))\n    88\t  .sort((a, b) => b.bytes - a.bytes);\n    89\tconst sum = modules.reduce((n, m) => n + m.bytes, 0);\n    90\tconst byDir = new Map();\n    91\tfor (const m of modules) { const d = m.file.includes(path.sep) ? m.file.split(path.sep)[0] : '(root)'; byDir.set(d, (byDir.get(d) ?? 0) + m.bytes); }\n    92\tconst dirs = [...byDir].sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]).map(([dir, bytes]) => ({ directory: dir, 'min B': bytes, share: `${((100 * bytes) / sum).toFixed(1)}%` }));\n    93\tconsole.log(`\\nPer-module breakdown of \"everything\" (source rebuilt with the release settings: ${rebuilt.outputFiles[0].contents.length} B minified; the packed axylo.js is ${packedProd} B, ${rebuilt.outputFiles[0].contents.length === packedProd ? 'identical in size' : `a difference of ${rebuilt.outputFiles[0].contents.length - packedProd} B, so dist was built from different source`}). Minified bytes before gzip; gzip does not split by module.`);\n    94\tconsole.table(dirs);\n    95\tconsole.table(modules.slice(0, 25).map(m => ({ module: m.file, 'min B': m.bytes, share: `${((100 * m.bytes) / sum).toFixed(1)}%` })));\n    96\t\n    97\tfs.mkdirSync(path.join(root, 'bench'), { recursive: true });\n    98\tfs.writeFileSync(path.join(root, 'bench/size-latest.json'), JSON.stringify({\n    99\t  date: new Date().toISOString(),\n   100\t  method: 'esbuild consumer bundles from tests/dist/fixtures (packed tarballs), minify, production condition; gzip level 9',\n   101\t  packed: { at: manifest.packedAt, axylo: manifest.packages.axylo.integrity, react: manifest.packages['@axylo/react'].integrity },\n   102\t  cssFileBytes: css.length, cssFileGzipBytes: gzip(css), rows,\n   103\t  breakdown: { rebuiltMinBytes: rebuilt.outputFiles[0].contents.length, packedMinBytes: packedProd, directories: dirs, modules },\n   104\t}, null, 1));\n   105\t\n   106\tif (process.argv.includes('--check') && rows.some(r => r.over)) {\n   107\t  console.error(`size target exceeded: ${rows.filter(r => r.over).map(r => `${r.entry} ${r.totalKiB} KiB > ${r.targetKiB} KiB`).join('; ')}`);\n   108\t  process.exit(1);\n   109\t}\n     1\t// Builds packages/axylo/dist: ESM (production and development flavours), script-tag files, CSS and type declarations.\n     2\timport { build } from 'esbuild';\n     3\timport { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process';\n     4\timport fs from 'node:fs';\n     5\tconst pkg = 'packages/axylo';\n     6\tfs.rmSync(`${pkg}/dist`, { recursive: true, force: true });\n     7\tconst common = { entryPoints: [`${pkg}/src/index.ts`], bundle: true, target: 'es2022', legalComments: 'none', logLevel: 'warning' };\n     8\tawait Promise.all([\n     9\t  build({ ...common, format: 'esm', minify: true, define: { __DEV__: 'false' }, outfile: `${pkg}/dist/axylo.js` }),\n    10\t  build({ ...common, format: 'esm', define: { __DEV__: 'true' }, outfile: `${pkg}/dist/axylo.dev.js` }),\n    11\t  build({ ...common, format: 'iife', globalName: 'Axylo', define: { __DEV__: 'true' }, outfile: `${pkg}/dist/axylo.iife.js` }),\n    12\t  build({ ...common, format: 'iife', globalName: 'Axylo', minify: true, define: { __DEV__: 'false' }, outfile: `${pkg}/dist/axylo.iife.min.js` }),\n    13\t]);\n    14\t// The stylesheet's source of truth is a string in the library (it is also applied at run time without a <style> element);\n    15\t// the standalone file is for pages that prefer to load it themselves, for example under a strict Content Security Policy.\n    16\t{\n    17\t  const r = await build({ entryPoints: [`${pkg}/src/theme/css.ts`], bundle: true, format: 'esm', write: false, logLevel: 'silent' });\n    18\t  const mod = await import('data:text/javascript;base64,' + Buffer.from(r.outputFiles[0].contents).toString('base64'));\n    19\t  fs.writeFileSync(`${pkg}/dist/axylo.css`, mod.AXYLO_CSS);\n    20\t}\n    21\texecFileSync('npx', ['tsc', '-p', `${pkg}/tsconfig.build.json`], { stdio: 'inherit' });\n    22\t// The React adapter needs the declarations just written.\n    23\texecFileSync('node', ['packages/react/build.mjs'], { stdio: 'inherit' });\n    24\tconsole.log('built', fs.readdirSync(`${pkg}/dist`).filter(f => !fs.statSync(`${pkg}/dist/${f}`).isDirectory()).join(', '));\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"name\": \"axylo\",\n     3\t  \"version\": \"0.0.0\",\n     4\t  \"description\": \"Everyday charts that are faithful to the data, readable everywhere and accessible by default.\",\n     5\t  \"license\": \"MIT\",\n     6\t  \"type\": \"module\",\n     7\t  \"sideEffects\": [\"*.css\"],\n     8\t  \"files\": [\n     9\t    \"dist/axylo.js\",\n    10\t    \"dist/axylo.dev.js\",\n    11\t    \"dist/axylo.iife.js\",\n    12\t    \"dist/axylo.iife.min.js\",\n    13\t    \"dist/axylo.css\",\n    14\t    \"dist/index.d.ts\",\n    15\t    \"dist/public.d.ts\"\n    16\t  ],\n    17\t  \"exports\": {\n    18\t    \".\": {\n    19\t      \"types\": \"./dist/index.d.ts\",\n    20\t      \"development\": \"./dist/axylo.dev.js\",\n    21\t      \"default\": \"./dist/axylo.js\"\n    22\t    },\n    23\t    \"./axylo.css\": \"./dist/axylo.css\"\n    24\t  }\n    25\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
