{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_59","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba docs/01-landscape.md | sed -n '5,22p;95,111p;158,185p;248,304p;310,338p;341,370p'; nl -ba research/harness/sizes.mjs | sed -n '1,125p'\"","aggregated_output":"     5\t## Summary\n     6\t\n     7\tTen widely used libraries were given the same reader-facing tasks and two load tasks, each with the least configuration it needs to complete the task. No configuration tested passed all of them. The gaps cluster in three places:\n     8\t\n     9\t1. **Nobody is told when the data is wrong.** Across five dirty-input fixtures, no library produced a message naming the field, the row or the problem. Nine of ten plotted the string `'40'` as a number without comment. A single `NaN` or `undefined` among valid numbers blanked the whole series in ApexCharts, broke Nivo, and broke either MUI X (`NaN`) or Highcharts (`undefined`, with an exception). Three libraries dropped category labels that did not fit, without saying so.\n    10\t2. **Accessibility depends on work the author rarely does.** Tested as the other nine were, Highcharts exposes one named image and no keyboard access; with its accessibility module imported it is the most complete of the ten. Three libraries (Recharts, ApexCharts, MUI X) had keyboard navigation on by default in the versions tested. Five exposed nothing a keyboard user could reach. Four keep animating bars under reduced motion. No default palette both reached 3:1 contrast and kept its colors apart under simulated color-vision deficiency.\n    11\t3. **Fast is something you configure.** Chart.js took 13.7 s of main-thread time to draw a million points with default options and 0.17 s in a tuned configuration. The fastest library by default, uPlot, needs hand-set sizes, colors and time units and has no accessibility layer.\n    12\t\n    13\tOne finding from the first version did not survive review: \"six of ten libraries shift dates\". Given instants, six show a daily value on a different day in some time zones, which is correct for instants. Given date strings, seven of ten keep the day everywhere. The real gap is narrower: nothing asks the author which one the data is.\n    14\t\n    15\tEvidence labels used below:\n    16\t\n    17\t| Label | Meaning |\n    18\t|---|---|\n    19\t| **Observed** | Seen in the harness in Chrome 157: rendered, probed or measured. The artifact is in `research/`. |\n    20\t| **Docs** | Read in the library's current documentation. Not tested unless also marked Observed. |\n    21\t| **Inferred** | A conclusion drawn from observed facts; the reasoning is stated. |\n    22\t| **Authored** | The result of code I wrote for the task (all of D3; parts of uPlot's bars and Recharts' colors), not of a library default. |\n    95\t### 1.4 Maintenance and cost facts\n    96\t\n    97\t| Library | Version tested | Published | Stable releases, 12 months | Major versions since Nov 2024 | License | Minified + gzipped, harness module | Harness lines |\n    98\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n    99\t| Chart.js | 4.6.1 | 2026-10-22 | 1 | none | MIT | 81 KiB | 54 |\n   100\t| Recharts | 3.11.0 | 2026-11-17 | 12 | v3 (2025-06) | MIT | 140 KiB + React | 83 + 9 shared |\n   101\t| D3 | 7.9.2 | 2026-11-03 | 2 | none | ISC | 35 KiB | 91 |\n   102\t| ECharts | 6.1.0 | 2026-05-19 | 1 | v6 (2025-07) | Apache-2.0 | 373 KiB | 63 |\n   103\t| Highcharts | 13.1.1 | 2026-09-20 | 10 | v12 (2024-11), v13 (2026-06) | Commercial | 143 KiB, accessibility module included | 51 |\n   104\t| ApexCharts | 8.9.0 | 2026-11-17 | 65 | v5 (2025-07), v6 (2026-07), v7 (2026-08), v8 (2026-10) | Free below a size threshold, otherwise commercial | 289 KiB | 60 |\n   105\t| Plotly.js | 4.3.0 | 2026-11-10 | 12 | v3 (2025-01), v4 (2026-08) | MIT | 1,450 KiB | 30 |\n   106\t| Nivo | 0.99.0 | 2025-05-23 | 0 | none | MIT | 129 KiB + React | 54 + 9 shared |\n   107\t| MUI X Charts | 9.18.1 | 2026-11-16 | 43 | v8 (2025-04), v9 (2026-04) | MIT; zoom and pan need the Pro plan | 160 KiB + React | 44 + 9 shared |\n   108\t| uPlot | 1.6.32 | 2025-03-14 | 0 | none | MIT | 23 KiB, no donut | 45 |\n   109\t\n   110\t**Observed** from the npm registry and from bundling each harness module with esbuild. The sizes and line counts are from the first probe pass and are not like for like: each size is the whole harness module (line, bar, donut where it exists, and the load tasks) through the import path the library's getting-started guide shows, with CSS counted as text. Most libraries document a smaller modular import that was not measured. Release counts describe package history; they do not measure how hard an upgrade is. License terms are **Docs** and were read once: the Highcharts shop listed 366 USD per seat for Highcharts Core; ApexCharts' license file sets a $2M threshold and has separate terms for embedding charts in a product, with details on its licensing page that I did not verify; MUI's accessibility page marks zoom and pan as Pro.\n   111\t\n   158\t| Visible chart title | built in | none | authored | built in | built in | built in | built in | none | none (accessible `title` only) | built in |\n   159\t| Donut: slice values readable without hovering | 0 of 6 | 6 of 6 (absent 1.8 s after creation, present at 4 s) | 4 of 6 (authored) | 0 of 6 (names only) | 0 of 6 (names only) | 4 of 6 (percent) | 6 of 6 (percent) | 6 of 6, two overlap | 0 of 6 | no donut |\n   160\t| Empty data | invented axes (0–1.0, hours) | blank frame | blank frame | blank | blank | invented axis (0–6) | invented axes (−1 to 6) | \"Jan 01\" eight times | **\"No data to display\"** | blank |\n   161\t\n   162\t### 3.2 Findings\n   163\t\n   164\t**R1. Zero baseline.** Nine of ten start bars at zero. uPlot has no bar chart type; with its bar path builder and the default y range, bars started at 80. That is a configuration a uPlot author would be expected to complete, and a place where a default can mislead.\n   165\t\n   166\t**R2. Labels that do not fit are the most common visible failure.** Only Highcharts wrapped \"Customer Success & Onboarding\". Recharts and MUI X removed that label and left a bar with no name; ECharts kept two labels of seven. Nivo, uPlot and the authored D3 chart overlapped text. ApexCharts and Plotly rotated the labels and they were cut at the container edge. Chart.js rotated all labels, and they stayed complete and readable.\n   167\t\n   168\t![Bars task](../research/harness/shots/_sheet-bars.png)\n   169\t\n   170\t**R3. Instants and dates are not told apart.** The pointer was moved to the same place with the browser in three zones. The cell shows the date each library displayed.\n   171\t\n   172\tGiven instants (epoch milliseconds at UTC midnight):\n   173\t\n   174\t| Library | Reader in UTC | Los Angeles | Kolkata | Same day everywhere |\n   175\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n   176\t| Chart.js | Feb 24, 12:00 a.m. | Feb 23, 4:00 p.m. | Feb 24, 5:30 a.m. | no |\n   177\t| ECharts | 2026-02-26 00:00 | 2026-02-25 16:00 | 2026-02-26 05:30 | no |\n   178\t| Plotly.js | Mar 10 | Mar 9 | Mar 10 | no |\n   179\t| Nivo | Sat Mar 07 00:00 | Fri Mar 06 16:00 | Sat Mar 07 05:30 | no |\n   180\t| MUI X Charts | Mon Feb 23 00:00 | Sun Feb 22 16:00 | Mon Feb 23 05:30 | no |\n   181\t| uPlot | 02-24 12:00am | 02-23 4:00pm | 02-24 5:30am | no |\n   182\t| Highcharts | Feb 24 | Feb 24 | Feb 24 | yes (UTC by default) |\n   183\t| ApexCharts | 24 Feb | 24 Feb | 24 Feb | yes (UTC by default) |\n   184\t| Recharts | decided by the author's formatter; the harness formatter showed the local date | | | – |\n   185\t| D3 (authored) | UTC scale chosen in the code | | | – |\n   248\t## 4. How readers explore and interact\n   249\t\n   250\t### 4.1 Scoreboard\n   251\t\n   252\tAll cells are **Observed** in Chrome's accessibility tree and by scripted keyboard and pointer input. No screen reader was run.\n   253\t\n   254\t| | Chart.js | Recharts | D3 (authored) | ECharts | Highcharts + module | ApexCharts | Plotly.js | Nivo | MUI X | uPlot |\n   255\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n   256\t| What the accessibility tree holds | nothing | legend list; an `application` element with no label, containing the tick text | image containing all the chart's text | nothing | named region, heading, generated description, a region per series | named `application`, legend buttons | two unnamed images, a toolbar | unnamed image | legend list, status region; the `title` label sits on a `role=\"none\"` element | title text, legend table |\n   257\t| Tab stops in the chart | 0 | 8, of which 7 land on unlabeled groups | 0 | 0 | 2 | 10 | 9: eight toolbar buttons and a link | 0 | 1 | 0 |\n   258\t| Arrow keys move between points | no | yes | no | no | yes | yes | no | no | yes | no |\n   259\t| Focused point's text reaches a live region | no | yes, `assertive` | no | no | no: tooltip only, the polite region stayed empty | yes, polite | no | no | no: tooltip only | no |\n   260\t| Focused point shown visually | – | not in the screenshot | – | – | yes | yes | – | – | yes | – |\n   261\t| Tooltip with the pointer between points | none | all series | all series (authored) | all series | one series | all series | one point | one point | all series | all series, in the legend |\n   262\t| Follows a container resize without code | yes | yes | scales like an image | **no** | yes | yes | **no** | yes | yes | **no** |\n   263\t| Bars animate on first render | yes | yes | no | yes | yes | yes | no | yes | yes | no |\n   264\t| …and stop under reduced motion | **no** | yes | – | **no** | **no** | yes | – | **no** | yes | – |\n   265\t\n   266\t![After Tab and four keys](../research/harness/shots/_sheet-kbd.png)\n   267\t\n   268\t### 4.2 Findings\n   269\t\n   270\t**R11. Half of the ten give a keyboard or assistive-technology user nothing to work with by default.** Chart.js and ECharts put one unlabeled canvas on the page. uPlot adds a title and a legend table that reads `--` until a mouse moves. Nivo is an unnamed image; the authored D3 chart is an image whose only content is its tick text. Chart.js' documentation leaves making the canvas accessible to the author (**Docs**, page dated 2021).\n   271\t\n   272\t**R12. Four configurations have keyboard navigation, with rough edges.** It was on by default in Recharts, ApexCharts and MUI X (the ApexCharts and MUI X documentation confirm the default), and in Highcharts once its module is imported. Observed rough edges:\n   273\t\n   274\t- Recharts and ApexCharts use `role=\"application\"`, which changes how screen readers pass keys through. Recharts' element has no label of its own. Leaving the Recharts chart took eight Tab presses.\n   275\t- ApexCharts has ten tab stops on this chart, six of them toolbar buttons for a zoomable time axis. **Inferred**: a dashboard of such charts multiplies that.\n   276\t- Highcharts and MUI X moved a visual focus marker and tooltip, but in this probe the point's text never reached a live region or the focused element's name. Whether a screen reader conveys it another way was not tested.\n   277\t- MUI X's documentation says the feature is still being developed (**Docs**).\n   278\t- Plotly's tab stops are its toolbar; the data cannot be reached.\n   279\t\n   280\t**R13. One configuration describes the chart.** Highcharts with its module generated: \"Line chart with 3 lines. The chart has 1 X axis displaying Time. Data ranges from 2026-01-01 00:00:00 to 2026-04-30 00:00:00. The chart has 1 Y axis displaying Sessions. Data ranges from 2732 to 48085.\" No tested configuration offers a data table without a further module.\n   281\t\n   282\t**R14. The bar entry animation ignores reduced motion in four of the seven that animate.** Chart.js, ECharts, Highcharts and Nivo produced the same early-frame difference with `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` as without. None of their packages contains that media query string ([`spot-checks.json`](../research/harness/spot-checks.json)). Recharts, ApexCharts and MUI X stop.\n   283\t\n   284\t**R15. Tooltips differ in basic behavior.** With the pointer in the plot between points, Chart.js shows nothing; its default requires the pointer to intersect a point. Highcharts, Plotly and Nivo describe one series or point; the others list all series at that x. MUI X's tooltip extended past the container's right edge.\n   285\t\n   286\t**R16. Three libraries do not follow their container.** ECharts, Plotly and uPlot stayed 720 px wide when the container shrank to 360 px. Each documents a resize call or option. uPlot needs pixel sizes up front.\n   287\t\n   288\t![Created at 340 px](../research/harness/shots/_sheet-narrow.png)\n   289\t\n   290\t### 4.3 Accessibility with each library's switch off and on\n   291\t\n   292\tThe first version compared Highcharts with its module against nine libraries without an equivalent. Both states, where a single documented switch exists:\n   293\t\n   294\t| Library | Switch | Off | On |\n   295\t|---|---|---|---|\n   296\t| Highcharts | Import `modules/accessibility` | One image named by the title; 0 tab stops; a console message recommends the module | Named region, generated description, keyboard navigation, 2 tab stops |\n   297\t| ECharts | `aria: { enabled: true }` (no extra import with the full build) | One unlabeled canvas | `role=\"img\"` with a generated label that lists the first ten items of each series, timestamps as raw numbers; still 0 tab stops |\n   298\t| MUI X Charts | `title` prop | Legend list and status region; no named chart | The label is set on an element with `role=\"none\"`, so no named chart appeared in the tree |\n   299\t| Recharts, ApexCharts | On by default | – | As in 4.1 |\n   300\t| Chart.js, Plotly.js, Nivo, uPlot, D3 | None found | As in 4.1 | – |\n   301\t\n   302\tSo \"Highcharts is the strongest\" holds only with its module, which is one import. By default it is in the weaker half.\n   303\t\n   304\t## 5. How developers create, update and maintain\n   310\t**Observed** while writing the harness ([`research/harness/src/libs/`](../research/harness/src/libs)). These are what one author needed, not proof that no shorter way exists.\n   311\t\n   312\t| Library | Had to know or add |\n   313\t|---|---|\n   314\t| Chart.js | A date adapter package before a time axis works. `maintainAspectRatio: false` to fill a fixed-height container. |\n   315\t| Recharts | Reshape the data into row objects. A color for every series. A tick formatter for a numeric time axis. Eight components for one line chart. |\n   316\t| D3 | Everything: title, legend, readout and axes are authored, with no keyboard access. |\n   317\t| ECharts | `tooltip` and `legend` are absent unless declared. |\n   318\t| Highcharts | The accessibility module is a separate import. |\n   319\t| ApexCharts | A numeric height: `height: '100%'` produced a 456 px chart in a 400 px container. |\n   320\t| Plotly.js | Nothing extra. The shortest module, and 1,450 KiB gzipped with the documented import. |\n   321\t| Nivo | Margins by hand, or axes are clipped. `useMesh` for a tooltip. A five-property object for a legend. A canvas line component exists and was not tested. |\n   322\t| MUI X Charts | `@mui/material` and `@mui/system` as peer dependencies. It also accepts row objects through a `dataset` prop, which the harness did not use. |\n   323\t| uPlot | Pixel width and height, a stroke color per series, x in seconds (or an option to accept milliseconds), a path builder plus a custom axis for bars. |\n   324\t\n   325\t### 5.2 Findings\n   326\t\n   327\t**D1. Line counts are similar; hidden knowledge is not.** What separates the libraries is the list above: things that give a broken or blank chart with no message when missed.\n   328\t\n   329\t**D2. Five data shapes for the same table.** Parallel arrays with labels (Chart.js, uPlot), `[x, y]` pairs per series (ECharts, Highcharts, ApexCharts), row objects keyed by series (Recharts), `{ id, data: [{ x, y }] }` (Nivo), and separate x and y arrays (Plotly, MUI X). The harness fixture is columnar and had to be reshaped a different way for each.\n   330\t\n   331\t**D3. Seven update idioms.** Mutate then `chart.update()` (Chart.js); `setOption` (ECharts); `series.addPoint` (Highcharts); `updateSeries` (ApexCharts); `Plotly.extendTraces`; `setData` (uPlot); re-render with new props (the React three).\n   332\t\n   333\t**D4. Bundle cost spans a wide range** with the documented imports: 23 KiB to 1,450 KiB gzipped for the harness modules (section 1.4, with its caveats).\n   334\t\n   335\t**D5. Errors do not help.** The one exception in the study came from minified code and named neither the option nor the data.\n   336\t\n   337\t**D6. Release cadence ranges from none to very frequent.** Nivo and uPlot have had no release in 18 and 20 months. ApexCharts shipped four major versions in 16 months. Whether either is a problem for a user depends on what broke, which this study did not test.\n   338\t\n   341\t### 5.3 Load\n   342\t\n   343\tTwo metrics from headless Chrome 157 on one desktop machine, median of three runs.\n   344\t\n   345\t- **Main-thread task time to draw**: time the browser reports its main thread busy, from the create call until 1.6 s after the second animation frame, long enough for entry animations to finish. It counts work however the library schedules it. The first row is the same measurement with no chart.\n   346\t- **Main-thread task time per update** while streaming: the same counter over 300 updates, divided by 300.\n   347\t\n   348\t| Library and configuration | 10,000 points | 100,000 points | 1,000,000 points | Streaming: main-thread time per update | Frame callbacks later than 20 ms (of 300) |\n   349\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n   350\t| No chart (measurement floor) | 8 ms | 8 ms | 9 ms | 0.1 ms | 0 |\n   351\t| Chart.js, defaults | 1.2 s | 2.3 s | 13.7 s | 10.7 ms | 2 |\n   352\t| Recharts, defaults | 2.5 s | 9.4 s | no result in 45 s | 15.2 ms | 17 |\n   353\t| D3 (authored: one SVG path, x axis redrawn) | 27 ms | 87 ms | 521 ms | 1.1 ms | 0 |\n   354\t| ECharts, defaults | 1.4 s | 2.8 s | 12.9 s | 15.7 ms | 30 |\n   355\t| Highcharts, defaults | 114 ms | 399 ms | 2.7 s | 3.5 ms | 0 |\n   356\t| ApexCharts, defaults | 83 ms | 134 ms | 636 ms | 9.7 ms | 3 |\n   357\t| Plotly.js, defaults | 51 ms | 119 ms | 365 ms | 6.1 ms | 0 |\n   358\t| Nivo, defaults | 733 ms | 5.0 s | no result in 45 s | 27.1 ms | 247 |\n   359\t| MUI X Charts, defaults | 99 ms | 366 ms | 3.0 s | 4.0 ms | 0 |\n   360\t| uPlot, defaults | 41 ms | 52 ms | 97 ms | 1.0 ms | 0 |\n   361\t| Chart.js, tuned configuration | 75 ms | 73 ms | 165 ms | 2.0 ms | 0 |\n   362\t| ECharts, tuned configuration | 65 ms | 119 ms | 512 ms | 2.2 ms | 0 |\n   363\t| Recharts, tuned configuration | 104 ms | 563 ms | 5.9 s | 4.7 ms | 0 |\n   364\t| Highcharts, Boost module (WebGL, software-rendered here) | 103 ms | 153 ms | 513 ms | 2.8 ms | 0 |\n   365\t| Plotly.js, `scattergl` (WebGL, software-rendered here) | 215 ms | 935 ms | 8.2 s | 17.3 ms | 23 |\n   366\t\n   367\tHow far these numbers can be trusted:\n   368\t\n   369\t- Three runs on one machine. Read them for order of magnitude.\n   370\t- Main-thread time does not include rasterization done on other threads, which matters for large SVG paths.\n     1\t// Bundle cost of each library for exactly the harness tasks (line + bar + donut): minified and gzipped.\n     2\t// React libraries are measured with react/react-dom external, since a React app already ships them.\n     3\timport { build } from 'esbuild';\n     4\timport { gzipSync, brotliCompressSync } from 'node:zlib';\n     5\timport fs from 'node:fs';\n     6\tconst libs = { chartjs: 'js', recharts: 'jsx', d3: 'js', echarts: 'js', highcharts: 'js', apexcharts: 'js', plotly: 'js', nivo: 'jsx', mui: 'jsx', uplot: 'js' };\n     7\tconst out = {};\n     8\tfor (const [lib, ext] of Object.entries(libs)) {\n     9\t  const r = await build({ entryPoints: [`src/libs/${lib}.${ext}`], bundle: true, minify: true, format: 'esm', write: false, jsx: 'automatic',\n    10\t    external: ['react', 'react-dom', 'react-dom/client', 'react/jsx-runtime'], loader: { '.css': 'text' }, define: { 'process.env.NODE_ENV': '\"production\"' }, logLevel: 'silent' });\n    11\t  const buf = Buffer.from(r.outputFiles[0].contents);\n    12\t  const src = fs.readFileSync(`src/libs/${lib}.${ext}`, 'utf8').split('\\n').filter(l => l.trim() && !l.trim().startsWith('//'));\n    13\t  out[lib] = { minKB: +(buf.length / 1024).toFixed(1), gzipKB: +(gzipSync(buf).length / 1024).toFixed(1), brotliKB: +(brotliCompressSync(buf).length / 1024).toFixed(1), harnessLoc: src.length };\n    14\t}\n    15\tfs.writeFileSync('sizes.json', JSON.stringify(out, null, 1));\n    16\tconsole.table(out);\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
